From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:39:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: s2r: Tell drivers that suspend-to-RAM powers the
 SoC down

Suspend-to-RAM takes the power away from every coprocessor, DART, SART
and PMGR power domain, and the drivers that have to restart those after
a sleep can't tell such a sleep from suspend-to-idle: nothing here sets
the PM core's firmware flags.

Set them as firmware-driven platforms do. From ->begin(),
pm_suspend_via_firmware() says before the drivers' suspend callbacks that
this sleep loses SoC state. pm_resume_via_firmware() is set only once
nothing is left that can abort before cpu_suspend(), so a sleep refused
by the RVBAR check or by a step leaves the drivers' state alone. A
finisher that comes back early still has them restart their blocks,
which is safe on a block that kept its state. The PM core clears both
flags at the start of every suspend.

This is what the coprocessor and DART suspend paths key on.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 drivers/soc/apple/apple-s2r.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/apple-s2r.c b/drivers/soc/apple/apple-s2r.c
index 48d08209c7..e1cb8b5ccc 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/apple-s2r.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/apple-s2r.c
@@ -31,6 +31,12 @@
  * The fabric and DCS performance states are staged by apple-pmgr-misc in its
  * noirq suspend callback, before this point.
  *
+ * From ->begin() the PM core reports the sleep as firmware-driven
+ * (pm_suspend_via_firmware()), so drivers whose block loses power in it can
+ * get ready for that. Once nothing is left that can abort before the
+ * commit, pm_resume_via_firmware() tells their resume callbacks to restart
+ * the block instead of assuming its state survived.
+ *
  * The SoC wakes the boot CPU at its reset vector. Boot firmware finds the
  * armed mailbox, redoes the boot CPU setup and branches to cpu_resume. After
  * the AIC syscore resume, this driver's syscore resume undoes the sequence:
@@ -522,6 +528,11 @@ static int apple_s2r_enter(suspend_state_t state)
 		s2r->wdt_armed = true;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * From here the SoC powers down: every block's resume has to assume
+	 * its state is gone, even if the finisher comes back early.
+	 */
+	pm_set_resume_via_firmware();
 	ret = cpu_suspend((unsigned long)s2r, apple_s2r_finish);
 	if (ret)
 		goto fail;
@@ -610,8 +621,19 @@ static void apple_s2r_wake(void)
 	s2r->restore_ret = 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Tell drivers before their suspend callbacks that this sleep powers the SoC
+ * down, as PSCI firmware does through pm_set_suspend_via_firmware().
+ */
+static int apple_s2r_begin(suspend_state_t state)
+{
+	pm_set_suspend_via_firmware();
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct platform_suspend_ops apple_s2r_ops = {
 	.valid = suspend_valid_only_mem,
+	.begin = apple_s2r_begin,
 	.enter = apple_s2r_enter,
 	.wake = apple_s2r_wake,
 };
